  "Question": "In how many ways can \\(47\\) be written as the sum of two primes?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(0\\)",
    "B": "\\(1\\)",
    "C": "\\(2\\)",
    "D": "\\(3\\)",
    "E": "more than \\(3\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "Because \\(47\\) is odd, its two summands must consist of one even number and one odd number. The only even prime is \\(2\\): every larger even number has \\(2\\) as a factor besides \\(1\\) and itself. Thus any possible pair of primes would have to be \\(2\\) and\n\\[\n47-2=45.\n\\]\nBut \\(45=5\\times9\\), so \\(45\\) is not prime. This rules out every possible pair, regardless of order. There are \\(0\\) ways, choice A.",
